{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-3300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bfe7ff027fd90ae1bbe73dc0a7c9c24c711a83b033688e4e82890f4015c3a926"} {"question" "q-3300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+12"] ["math" "12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+9,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bfe7ff027fd90ae1bbe73dc0a7c9c24c711a83b033688e4e82890f4015c3a926"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{f}^{-1}(x)"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{f}^{-1}(x)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{{f}^{-1}(x)}"] ["math" "\\frac{{f}^{-1}(x)}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-3302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+6y-2\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+6y-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:**\n\nLastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bfe7ff027fd90ae1bbe73dc0a7c9c24c711a83b033688e4e82890f4015c3a926"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}"] ["math" "4{g}^{-1}(x)+6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2({g}^{-1}(x))+6{g}^{-1}(x)-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2{g}^{-1}(x)+6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-32629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}) \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bfe7ff027fd90ae1bbe73dc0a7c9c24c711a83b033688e4e82890f4015c3a926"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})"] ["math" "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})"] ["math" "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})"] ["math" "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})"] ["math" "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-51349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-73083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $g(x)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-73083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡2y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-73083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bfe7ff027fd90ae1bbe73dc0a7c9c24c711a83b033688e4e82890f4015c3a926"} {"question" "q-73083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990962/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a85cb46435b37b8c1c7caac571536d05623f2f1e0643cb377eadc642a961cc8"} {"question" "q-73083" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({g}^{-1}(x